  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Tue Apr 21 2026\n\nToday through Thursday: Warm and dry conditions will be the \ngeneral rule through mid week as upper level ridging brings \nsubsident, anticyclonic flow. There will be an uptick in fire \ndanger today (limited to elevated fire danger threat) due to a \nvery deep mixing layer that could support gusty winds during peak \nheating and critically low RH value, so we will continue to \nmessage this formally in the HWO graphics. Similar conditions and \nthe potential for additional messaging will exist for Wed-Thu, \nalthough RH should not be quite as low as today. \n\nFriday through next Monday: An increasingly active subtropical \njet stream continues to show up in the global model guidance for \nthe end of this week into the weekend, and now into early next \nweek as well. Per previous forecast discussion, we continue to \napproach rainfall forecasts with caution in our current drought \npattern given the recent errant and overly aggressive guidance, \nbut it appears there is greater support for getting a few rounds \nof showers/storms in the Fri-Mon time frame. \n\nPer recent NCAR/AI guidance, a general low end risk for severe \nweather continues to be indicated in our region during the Fri-Mon\ntime frame, and SPC has explicitly outlooked our area for Monday \n(Day 7). With northwest portions of the area seeming to be a\ntarget area this Friday aftn/evening for organized convection in \nmuch of the recent global guidance, it would not be surprising if \na low end severe risk ultimately ends up being advertised there. \nAs for the day 7 severe risk, will hold off on mentioning this in \nthe graphics given the continued shaky confidence in the details\nat that time range. /EC/",
